{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310714,"nonce":"fs0LAOtTv1WR4xCxTJQoE/NB4N8=","title":"MEASURED: cached stdlib artifacts are byte-identical across programs — the cross-program miscompile is a RESTORE-vs-COMPILE asymmetry, not ...","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-17 at `43d0d890`. This overturns the theory in the comment\nat the `user_program_hash()` fold (compilation.rs ~1157) and in\n[[bugs-nongeneric-stdlib-modules-still-program-dependent]].**\n\nMethod: env-gated the fold off for stdlib sources, compiled two different\nprograms in separate dirs, byte-compared every `.blade`.\n\n**THE ARTIFACTS ARE IDENTICAL.** hello (`Sys.println`) vs a Map/StringTools\nprogram: 42/42 identical. `Channel\u003cInt\u003e` inferred vs `Channel\u003cSomeClass\u003e`\ninferred — the exact instantiation difference blamed for the miscompile:\n**46/46 identical.**\n\n**TIMESTAMP TRAP:** a naive diff shows 12 modules \"differing\" by exactly 2\nbytes. Those are `source_timestamp`/`compile_timestamp`, two 5-byte LEB128\nvarints right after the source hash, both set to *now* — one second apart\nbetween sequential runs. Normalise them before comparing or you will chase\nghosts. (Memory warned a previous byte-diff analysis was wrong; this is the\nopposite error — differences that are NOT real.)\n\n**SO THE CAUSE IS RESTORE, NOT THE ARTIFACT.** Sharing ON reproduces\n`test_sys_thread` failing 2 tests; sharing OFF passes; the artifacts either\nway are the same bytes. What differs is that restoring a module is not\nequivalent to compiling it. `compilation.rs:3198` already says so (\"BLADE\npre-registers symbols but doesn't preserve full TAST state needed for GENERIC\nINSTANTIATION and method resolution\") — that comment is the real statement of\nthe problem, and the cache key is a workaround for it.\n\nThe observable failure: `sys.thread.Thread.join` lowers to `rayzor_thread_join`\ninstead of `sys_thread_join`. Both classes have `join`, so it is silent.\n\n**TWO REAL BUGS FOUND, NEITHER SHIPPED:**\n\n1. **`class_key` silently picks among ambiguous spellings.** It is\n   `candidates(spelling).first()`, and exactly three spellings are shared:\n   `Thread` → [rayzor.concurrent.Thread, sys.thread.Thread], `Mutex`, `Socket`.\n   `rayzor.` sorts before `sys.`, so bare `Thread` always means the rayzor one.\n   **DO NOT \"fix\" this by refusing ambiguity** — tried it, and it breaks 5\n   tests (debug_channel, test_channel_inferred_{int,class}_threaded,\n   test_channel_init_class): those callers hold only the bare name and\n   legitimately mean rayzor.concurrent.Thread, so the first-match pick is\n   LOAD-BEARING. The real fix is to give those call sites the qualified name;\n   only then can the resolver refuse.\n\n2. **Module-private types are registered globally.** `parser/src/haxe_ast.rs`\n   `enum Modifier` has NO `Private` variant (only Static/Inline/Macro/Dynamic/\n   Override/Final/Extern), so `private class Thread` in\n   `haxe-std/haxe/EntryPoint.hx` registers as `haxe.Thread`. **33 module-private\n   type declarations across haxe-std** are public this way. A receiver in a file\n   that does `import sys.thread.Thread` gets `qualified_name = \"haxe.Thread\"` —\n   a stub whose `create(f)` just calls `f()` and which has no `join` at all.\n   NOT PROVEN to cause the sharing failure (it is present in the passing run\n   too), but it is wrong on its own and it is why `Thread` is ambiguous at all.\n\n**THE FAILURES ARE CONFIG-SPECIFIC, NOT PROGRAM-SPECIFIC — narrowed 2026-08-17.**\nWith sharing on:\n\n| harness | config | processes | result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `run_haxe_tests.sh` (184 programs) | CLI default | one per program | **184/184 PASS** |\n| `test_sys_thread`, `test_rayzor_stdlib_e2e` | `CompilationConfig::fast()` (lazy stdlib) | many programs, ONE process, shared on-disk cache | FAIL |\n\n**And every failing program is CORRECT standalone, in BOTH modes.** Extracted\n`thread_create_basic` verbatim → prints `true` with sharing on and off, and\n`join` resolves identically (`class=rayzor.concurrent.Thread -\u003e Thread_join`)\neither way. Extracted `channel_while_loop` (the stack overflow) → runs clean\nboth ways. So neither failure is a miscompile of the program; they only appear\nunder that harness's configuration.\n\nThe `test_sys_thread` failures are the harness asserting `sys_thread_join` is\nin the module's function list. The program works; the symbol just arrives via\na restored module instead of a compiled one. `e4ec2b11` already records these\nassertions flip-flopping — treat them as a statement about WHICH module holds\na symbol, not about correctness, and confirm behaviour standalone first.\n\n**SO THE OPEN QUESTION NARROWED:** what does `CompilationConfig::fast()` (lazy\nstdlib loading) fail to register that the CLI config registers, such that a\nRESTORED module is fine under one and not the other? That is a much smaller\nquestion than \"is cross-program sharing sound\".\n\n**NEXT:** the question to answer is what the restore path fails to reconstruct,\nnot which modules to exclude. `RAYZOR_STRICT_BLADE` counts dropped references;\nstart there, with sharing ON, and diff restored vs freshly-compiled symbol\nstate for `sys.thread.Thread`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_stdlib_artifacts_are_identical_restore_is_not` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}